{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-code:TokenMixer --start-line 112 --lines 107\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-benchmark-v2 --lines 80\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve framework:unirank/pytorch/models/rank_model.py\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read framework:unirank/pytorch/models/rank_model.py --find get_inputs --lines 95'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:TokenMixer\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/TokenMixer.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"fa106d28184521fd2cbbfeddeada4fb756534651eb74a24639e6a0e8386f3bba\",\n  \"total_lines\": 218,\n  \"start_line\": 112,\n  \"next_line\": 219,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"112:     def forward(self, inputs):\\n113:         batch_dict, item_dict, mask = self.get_inputs(inputs)\\n114:         batch_size = mask.shape[0]\\n115:         # item_dict contains [history_items..., target_item]\\n116:         # Reshape flattened embeddings to B x (T+1) x item_info_dim\\n117:         item_seq_emb = self.embedding_layer(item_dict, flatten_emb=True)\\n118:         item_seq_emb = item_seq_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.item_info_dim)\\n119: \\n120:         target_emb = item_seq_emb[:, -1, :]      # B x item_info_dim\\n121:         sequence_emb = item_seq_emb[:, 0:-1, :]  # B x T x item_info_dim\\n122: \\n123:         seq_pooling_emb = self.attention_layers(target_emb, sequence_emb, mask) # B x item_info_dim\\n124: \\n125:         # Other non-sequential features -> NS tokens\\n126:         user_context_emb = self.embedding_layer(batch_dict, flatten_emb=False)       # B x F × embedding_dim\\n127:         feature_embeddings = torch.cat([user_context_emb, target_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.embedding_dim),\\n128:                                         seq_pooling_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.embedding_dim)], dim=1)\\n129:         unified_tokens = self.unified_tokenizer_layer(feature_embeddings)  # B x (1 + num_group_token) x token_dim\\n130: \\n131: \\n132:         # unified model\\n133:         unified_tokens = self.activation_checkpoint(\\n134:             self.unified_interaction_layers,\\n135:             unified_tokens\\n136:         )\\n137: \\n138:         bottom_output = unified_tokens.mean(dim=1)\\n139:         tower_output = [self.tower[i](bottom_output) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n140:         y_pred = [self.output_activation[i](tower_output[i]) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n141:         return_dict = {}\\n142:         labels = self.feature_map.labels\\n143:         for i in range(self.num_tasks):\\n144:             return_dict[\\\"{}_pred\\\".format(labels[i])] = y_pred[i]\\n145:         return return_dict\\n146: \\n147: \\n148: class TokenMixerTokenizer(nn.Module):\\n149:     def __init__(self, embedding_dim, token_dim, num_group_token, num_field):\\n150:         super(TokenMixerTokenizer, self).__init__()\\n151:         input_dim = num_field * embedding_dim\\n152:         self.global_token_mlps = AutoSplitTokenizer(input_dim, token_dim, 1)\\n153:         self.group_token_mlps = ChunkTokenizer(input_dim, token_dim, num_group_token)\\n154: \\n155:     def forward(self, feature_embeddings):\\n156:         global_tokens = self.global_token_mlps(feature_embeddings)\\n157:         group_tokens = self.group_token_mlps(feature_embeddings)\\n158:         return torch.cat([global_tokens, group_tokens], dim=1)\\n159: \\n160: \\n161: class TokenMixerBlocks(nn.Module):\\n162:     def __init__(self,\\n163:                  input_dim,\\n164:                  num_tokens,\\n165:                  num_layers,\\n166:                  expand=2,\\n167:                  net_dropout=0.0):\\n168:         super(TokenMixerBlocks, self).__init__()\\n169:         self.num_layers = num_layers\\n170:         self.input_dim = input_dim\\n171:         self.num_tokens = num_tokens\\n172:         self.block_dim = ((input_dim + num_tokens - 1) // num_tokens) * num_tokens\\n173:         self.pad_dim = self.block_dim - input_dim\\n174:         self.output_dim = self.block_dim\\n175: \\n176:         self.mixer_norms = nn.ModuleList([\\n177:             nn.LayerNorm(self.block_dim)\\n178:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n179:         ])\\n180:         self.pffn_norms = nn.ModuleList([\\n181:             nn.LayerNorm(self.block_dim)\\n182:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n183:         ])\\n184: \\n185:         self.mixer_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n186:             MultiHeadTokenMixing(input_dim=self.block_dim, num_token=num_tokens)\\n187:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n188:         ])\\n189:         self.revert_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n190:             MultiHeadTokenMixing(input_dim=self.block_dim, num_token=num_tokens)\\n191:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n192:         ])\\n193:         self.pffn_layers1 = nn.ModuleList([\\n194:             PerTokenSwiGLU(input_dim=self.block_dim,\\n195:                            num_token=num_tokens,\\n196:                            expand=expand,\\n197:                            net_dropout=net_dropout)\\n198:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n199:         ])\\n200:         self.pffn_layers2 = nn.ModuleList([\\n201:             PerTokenSwiGLU(input_dim=self.block_dim,\\n202:                                 num_token=num_tokens,\\n203:                                 expand=expand,\\n204:                                 net_dropout=net_dropout)\\n205:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n206:         ])\\n207: \\n208:     def forward(self, x: torch.Tensor): # B x T x token_dim\\n209:         if x.size(1) != self.num_tokens:\\n210:             raise ValueError(f\\\"TokenMixerBlocks expects {self.num_tokens} tokens, got {x.size(1)}.\\\")\\n211:         if self.pad_dim > 0:\\n212:             x = F.pad(x, (0, self.pad_dim))\\n213:         for i in range(self.num_layers):\\n214:             mix_x = self.mixer_layers[i](self.mixer_norms[i](x))\\n215:             mix_x = self.pffn_layers1[i](mix_x)\\n216:             x = self.revert_layers[i](mix_x) + x\\n217:             x = self.pffn_layers2[i](self.pffn_norms[i](x)) + x\\n218:         return x\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2607.19987v2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Cached section2.3 method descriptions; not an original-method full-paper audit.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"34ff9c412b35f3118aae377faca5f658d7bffac0b4ef1c12a4eb0c7937372789\",\n  \"total_lines\": 39,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 40,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\\n2: Version: arxiv:2607.19987v2\\n3: Scope: section2.3 only, original benchmark method descriptions.\\n4: \\n5: 2.3. Representative Models\\n6: 2.3.1. Stacked Unified Interaction\\n7: This paradigm arranges sequential modeling and feature interaction as consecutive modules. The sequence module first encodes the behavioral history, and the feature interaction module then combines this representation with user, target-item, and context features.\\n8: •\\n9: HiFormer (Gui et al., 2023) pools the behavior history with target attention, tokenizes heterogeneous fields and task queries, and applies hierarchical HiFormer blocks to the stacked tokens.\\n10: •\\n11: RankMixer (Zhu et al., 2025) pools the history with target attention, tokenizes concatenated ranking fields into chunks, and alternates parameter-free Token Mixing with per-token MLPs.\\n12: •\\n13: Zenith (Zhang et al., 2026) separately embeds user and item identifiers, pools historical item attributes with target attention, tokenizes context and target attributes, and applies Zenith interaction blocks.\\n14: •\\n15: TokenMixer (Jiang et al., 2026) combines target-aware sequence summaries with global and group tokens, then applies reversible token mixing and per-token feed-forward networks.\\n16: •\\n17: UniMixer (Ha et al., 2026) flattens histories with user and context fields, then chunk-tokenizes them for UniMixing-Lite blocks.\\n18: •\\n19: HeMix (Wang et al., 2026) combines fixed and feature-conditioned queries with low-rank token mixing over recent and earlier histories.\\n20: •\\n21: SSR (Yu et al., 2026) uses target-attention sequence pooling and multi-view competitive sparse filters with dense fusion.\\n22: 2.3.2. Layer-wise Unified Interaction\\n23: This paradigm integrates sequential and non-sequential representations throughout every network layer.\\n24: •\\n25: OneTrans (Zhang et al., 2025) injects sequence and partitioned non-sequential tokens into every block, using mixed attention, a shared sequence FFN, and token-specific FFNs.\\n26: •\\n27: HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026b) encodes the sequence per layer, decodes global queries, and enriches them with non-sequential tokens.\\n28: •\\n29: MixFormer (Huang et al., 2026a) mixes non-sequential queries, uses cross-attention to retrieve information from the updated sequence, and fuses the resulting representations.\\n30: •\\n31: INFNet (Li et al., 2025b) organizes categorical, sequential, and task tokens around a hub for layer-wise aggregation and broadcasting.\\n32: •\\n33: EST (Liu et al., 2026) uses cross-attention to sequence representations and feature-specific feed-forward layers.\\n34: •\\n35: TokenFormer (Zhou et al., 2026) represents fields, histories, and targets as tokens with full interaction and sliding-window Flex Attention.\\n36: •\\n37: LONGER (Chai et al., 2025) encodes local groups with inner Transformers and joins compressed long-term history with recent queries.\\n38: •\\n39: UltraHSTU (Ding et al., 2026) extends HSTU-style sequential transduction (Zhai et al., 2024) with hybrid attention that combines sparse and full attention in each interaction layer.\"\n}\n{\n  \"source\": \"primary upstream implementation\",\n  \"path\": \"unirank/pytorch/models/rank_model.py\",\n  \"version\": \"90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"content_sha256\": \"c729cd9d20b22a4a2f0120325ddff9a1528206519f514e5e02387e6b1b3dfb9f\",\n  \"scope\": \"read-only API reference; does not prescribe a model\",\n  \"text_ref\": \"source-text://framework:unirank/pytorch/models/rank_model.py\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"c729cd9d20b22a4a2f0120325ddff9a1528206519f514e5e02387e6b1b3dfb9f\",\n  \"text_lines\": 890\n}\n{\n  \"id\": null,\n  \"url\": null,\n  \"version\": \"90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"access_scope\": null,\n  \"text_sha256\": \"c729cd9d20b22a4a2f0120325ddff9a1528206519f514e5e02387e6b1b3dfb9f\",\n  \"total_lines\": 890,\n  \"start_line\": 323,\n  \"next_line\": 418,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"323:         self.apply(custom_reset_params)\\n324: \\n325:     def get_inputs(self, inputs, feature_source=None, return_multi_masks=False):\\n326:         \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n327:         Args:\\n328:             inputs: (batch_dict, item_dict, mask) or (batch_dict, item_dict, mask, multi_masks)\\n329:             feature_source: optional feature source filtering\\n330:             return_multi_masks: bool, default False\\n331:                 - False: Return (X_dict, item_dict, mask)\\n332:                 - True : Returns (X_dict, item_dict, mask, multi_masks)\\n333:         \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n334:         if len(inputs) == 4:\\n335:             batch_dict, item_dict, mask, multi_masks = inputs\\n336:         elif len(inputs) == 3:\\n337:             batch_dict, item_dict, mask = inputs\\n338:             multi_masks = None\\n339:         else:\\n340:             raise ValueError(f\\\"Unexpected inputs length: {len(inputs)}. Expected 3 or 4.\\\")\\n341: \\n342:         X_dict = dict()\\n343:         for feature, value in batch_dict.items():\\n344:             if feature in self.feature_map.labels:\\n345:                 continue\\n346:             feature_spec = self.feature_map.features[feature]\\n347:             if feature_spec[\\\"type\\\"] == \\\"meta\\\":\\n348:                 continue\\n349:             if feature_source and not_in_whitelist(feature_spec[\\\"source\\\"], feature_source):\\n350:                 continue\\n351:             X_dict[feature] = value.to(self.device)\\n352: \\n353:         for item, value in item_dict.items():\\n354:             item_dict[item] = value.to(self.device)\\n355: \\n356:         mask = mask.to(self.device)\\n357: \\n358:         if not return_multi_masks:\\n359:             return X_dict, item_dict, mask\\n360: \\n361:         if multi_masks is None:\\n362:             multi_masks = [mask for _ in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n363:         else:\\n364:             if isinstance(multi_masks, (list, tuple)):\\n365:                 multi_masks = [m.to(self.device) for m in multi_masks]\\n366:             elif torch.is_tensor(multi_masks):\\n367:                 multi_masks = multi_masks.to(self.device)\\n368:                 if multi_masks.dim() == 3 and multi_masks.size(0) == self.num_tasks:\\n369:                     multi_masks = [multi_masks[i] for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n370:                 else:\\n371:                     multi_masks = [multi_masks for _ in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n372:             else:\\n373:                 multi_masks = [mask for _ in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n374:         return X_dict, item_dict, mask, multi_masks\\n375: \\n376:     def get_labels(self, inputs):\\n377:         \\\"\\\"\\\" Please override get_labels() when using multiple labels!\\n378:         \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n379:         labels = self.feature_map.labels\\n380:         y = inputs[labels[0]].to(self.device)\\n381:         return y.float().view(-1, 1)\\n382: \\n383:     def get_group_id(self, inputs):\\n384:         return inputs[0][self.feature_map.group_id]\\n385: \\n386:     def model_to_device(self):\\n387:         self.to(device=self.device)\\n388:         self._optimizer_to_device()\\n389: \\n390:     def _optimizer_to_device(self):\\n391:         if not getattr(self, \\\"optimizers\\\", None):\\n392:             return\\n393:         for optimizer in self._iter_optimizers():\\n394:             for param, state in optimizer.state.items():\\n395:                 param_device = param.device\\n396:                 for key, value in state.items():\\n397:                     if torch.is_tensor(value):\\n398:                         state[key] = value.to(device=param_device)\\n399: \\n400:     def lr_decay(self, factor=0.1, min_lr=1e-6):\\n401:         dense_lr = None\\n402:         for optimizer in self._iter_optimizers():\\n403:             for param_group in optimizer.param_groups:\\n404:                 reduced_lr = max(param_group[\\\"lr\\\"] * factor, min_lr)\\n405:                 param_group[\\\"lr\\\"] = reduced_lr\\n406:                 if optimizer is self.dense_optimizer and dense_lr is None:\\n407:                     dense_lr = reduced_lr\\n408:         if dense_lr is not None:\\n409:             return dense_lr\\n410:         return self._iter_optimizers()[0].param_groups[0][\\\"lr\\\"]\\n411: \\n412:     def fit(self, data_generator, epochs=1, validation_data=None,\\n413:             max_gradient_norm=10., **kwargs):\\n414:         \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n415:         DDP + blocked dataloader training instructions:\\n416:         - When blocked=True, the local batch numbers of different ranks may not be completely consistent;\\n417:         - During the DDP training phase, if some ranks enter eval/barrier first, while other ranks are still in backward allreduce,\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nr=json.loads(Path('runtime_view.json').read_text())\nprotocol=r['required_study_protocol_ref']\nshared={'embedding_dim':16,'token_dim':32,'num_heads':2,'num_layers':2,'field_retrieval':True,'feedback_groups':False,'tower_hidden_units':[128,64]}\nspecs=[('mass_sum','candidate.py','candidate','sigmoid_sum'),('mass_normalized','mass_normalized.py','control','sigmoid_normalized'),('count_scaled','count_scaled.py','control','sigmoid_count'),('softmax_reference','softmax_reference.py','control','softmax')]\nstudy={\n'id':'g0-r0001-field-activation-mass',\n'protocol_ref':protocol,\n'evidence_refs':['baseline-din','g0-r0000-field_feedback','g0-r0000-event_feedback','g0-r0000-field_global','g0-r0000-event_global'],\n'question':'Does preserving target/context-conditioned activation mass in field-wise history summary units recover click discrimination lost by unit-normalized summaries, beyond history count and a different attention kernel?',\n'hypothesis':('Observed: all four preceding single-seed variants execute successfully but trail baseline-din click AUC 0.655544. Field retrieval improves event retrieval by 0.000528 with feedback grouping and 0.001201 without it. Grouping lowers click AUC by 0.005860 (field) and 0.005187 (event), despite improved auxiliary task AUCs over DIN. The best preceding unified variant g0-r0000-field_global reaches 0.646474, with history_1_16 AUC 0.591524 versus DIN 0.614321 and history_gt64 0.653470 versus DIN 0.657803. No prior candidate diagnostics or repetitions establish why. Code inspection shows each field summary is normalized to unit total weight and separately receives log exposure/group counts. Hypothesis: counts do not preserve target-dependent activation mass. A field summary represented as a bounded gated sum may retain both content and target-conditioned magnitude, improving click ranking. This is a provisional explanation, not a conclusion from those unequal-architecture comparisons. DIN already motivates unnormalized pooling; the contribution sought here is controlled local evidence about field summary representation, not invention of that idea.'),\n'nearest_work':[\n {'source_ref':'paper:din','verification':'read','difference':'Archived exact-version primary text, lines 250-277, explicitly motivates unnormalized local activation to retain interest intensity. Inherit that known motivation; use bounded sigmoid dot-product gates for each field with target/context queries in the existing unified token model, rather than DIN signed MLP event activations. No originality claim for unnormalized pooling or exact DIN reproduction.'},\n {'source_ref':'unirank-code:HiFormer','verification':'implementation_checked','difference':'Pinned benchmark implementation lines 80-145 pools whole item/action events, tokenizes pooled fields and task tokens, then uses HiFormer blocks. Keep our preceding field-specific context queries, shared small attention blocks and direct summary/static readout; vary only retrieval normalization. Original HiFormer paper detail unavailable through paper:hiformer, so equivalence and priority remain unverified.'},\n {'source_ref':'unirank-code:TokenMixer','verification':'implementation_checked','difference':'Pinned benchmark implementation lines 112-158 uses target-pooled fields with global/chunk group tokens, reversible mixing and mean readout. No reversible transport or global/group tokenizer is adopted. It is a considered architectural alternative rather than a locally measured control. Original method text unavailable through paper:tokenmixer.'},\n {'source_ref':'trial://g0-r0000-field_global','verification':'implementation_checked','difference':'Exact local architectural parent. All embeddings, queries, keys, five summary slots, group counts/absence flags, 32-dimensional projections, two attention blocks, task identities, direct static/summary path and towers remain. Only the deterministic mapping of existing field retrieval scores to weights changes. softmax_reference is a prospective faithful rerun.'}\n],\n'inherited_components':[\n 'g0-r0000-field_global entire model and initialization order; inherited source SHA256 a3b2aa2e254ec5d995d9a4361592af40fb25a9c1c09023bc7d1223d2b5ccc125',\n 'Frozen FeatureEmbeddingDict, uniform dimension 16, target/context field queries and shared keys, token dimension 32, two heads and two interaction blocks',\n 'Five global retrieval summary slots, original feedback-group presence gating and group log counts, static field tokens, four task tokens and four Dice towers [128,64]',\n 'Frozen from-scratch single-pass recipe, four unweighted BCEs, fixed inputs/history masks; no custom loss, extra features, positions, labels or external data',\n 'Known DIN activation-intensity motivation; no claim to reproduce original DIN or any industry method'\n],\n'proposed_difference':('For existing score s[b,f,t], define g=sigmoid(s) times the original binary retrieval mask, M=sum_t g, N=sum_t mask, and v=sum_t g*x. mass_sum uses v; mass_normalized uses v/max(M,1e-9); count_scaled uses (N/2)*v/max(M,1e-9); softmax_reference retains the exact original masked softmax. In all arms every field has its own gate and pooled content. The action embedding uses the average field weights, exactly as before. Count-scaled pooling has mass N/2, equal to sigmoid-sum mass at score zero, providing a target-independent magnitude control. Gate-normalized and sum arms use the same sigmoid kernel and tensor shapes. No new trainable tensors: expected parameters 32110916 total / 31793216 embeddings / 317700 dense, to be measured by the host. Field retrieval stays true and feedback_groups false in all arms. Existing group absence flags, counts, repeated summary slots and zero-length paths stay unchanged to avoid introducing another intervention. This narrowly tests a representation property after reviewing a failed direction; it does not establish a new unified architecture or isolate the earlier grouping failure.'),\n'predictions':[\n 'Primary prospective contrast: mass_sum should improve full-population validation click AUC over count_scaled if target-dependent mass contributes beyond history-count magnitude. A difference of at least +0.001 is the predeclared practical follow-up threshold; one seed is preliminary and a smaller effect is inconclusive rather than a mechanism refutation.',\n 'mass_sum versus mass_normalized tests retaining the sigmoid gate mass; count_scaled versus mass_normalized tests count-linked magnitude; mass_normalized versus softmax_reference tests kernel replacement. Interpret these jointly, rather than labeling every sum/mean gain target-intensity evidence.',\n 'Inspect inherited fixed history bins (empty,1-16,17-64,>64), recording N/positive/negative counts from the authorized host output. Magnitude effects should be visible in at least one nonempty bin, but which bin benefits is exploratory. Empty histories have no retrieval contribution in any arm; differences there can arise through shared learned parameters and Dice statistics.',\n 'A candidate must also be compared with baseline-din 0.655544 AUC and g0-r0000-field_global 0.646474. Better auxiliary mean AUC cannot replace click selection. Report all task AUC/Logloss, memory, training and validation time, allocated GPU seconds when supplied, examples processed, throughput and latency; these costs remain unmeasured for the new arms.',\n 'softmax_reference should reproduce the parent within host numerical reproducibility tolerance. If it does not, investigate source/config/data/protocol identity before pooling attribution.'\n],\n'falsifiers':[\n 'If mass_sum fails to exceed count_scaled on click AUC, this implementation provides no support for useful target-dependent magnitude beyond count. If it also fails mass_normalized, there is no support for removing normalization here. One failed bounded gate implementation does not refute all intensity-preserving models.',\n 'If only mass_normalized beats softmax_reference while mass_sum and count_scaled do not improve it, the observed gain is compatible with kernel replacement rather than retained activation mass.',\n 'If count_scaled matches or beats mass_sum and both beat mass_normalized, results support count-linked scaling/optimization, not specifically target-conditioned intensity.',\n 'A softmax reference mismatch, unequal parameter counts, unauthorized inputs, nonfinite probabilities/gradients, or cap failures prevents causal interpretation until resolved within the same contracts. Preserve failed receipts.',\n 'Even if a single-seed contrast exceeds +0.001, lack of paired repetition or user-cluster uncertainty leaves only local preliminary evidence. Inferential significance cannot be claimed from slice counts or repeated exposures alone.'\n],\n'alternative_explanations':[\n 'Sigmoid changes attention selectivity and score gradients relative to softmax; the normalized-sigmoid control tests this alternative.',\n 'Magnitude may mainly encode history length, already separately supplied as counts; the N/2 scale control isolates a count-linked alternative at the zero-score operating point.',\n 'Sum versus normalization changes gradient scale, sigmoid saturation, BF16 rounding, Dice statistics and finite-one-pass optimization. Equal parameters and equal data passes do not eliminate these alternatives; no loss reweighting, optimizer change or per-arm hyperparameter tuning is permitted.',\n 'The 112-to-32 projection (for six item fields plus action), downstream interaction, lack of sequence positions, shared field retrieval and static context processing may be responsible for the earlier DIN gap. None is changed or ruled out here.',\n 'The short-history gap and grouping penalty may reflect task competition, rare groups, auxiliary label semantics or seeds. Current evidence does not establish those causes, and the subgrouped branch remains a recorded negative trajectory.',\n 'Earlier knowledge usage declared EST-style field identity consideration but did not implement iterative event-state updates; those prior runs support at most a small field-retrieval effect and do not validate EST or knowledge usefulness. Previously rejected Zenith/UltraHSTU/SSR directions remain unevaluated, not scientifically refuted.',\n 'Official offline history visibility, whole-window vocabularies/static snapshots and category/brand-based auxiliary labels limit production and advertisement-conversion interpretations.'\n],\n'contrasts':[],\n'cost_plan':{\n 'estimate_ref':'g0-r0000-field_global wall 325.231653s, training 293.755228s, inference 25.294572s; baseline-din wall 328.913273s; runtime protocol conservative estimated_unit_seconds 481.347588 and trial cap 1203.368969s. Remaining research wall at contract resolution 169076.881228s; all subsequent preparation and scoring costs count.',\n 'all_variants_counted':True,\n 'attribution_reserve':'Four full-protocol runs, no subset screening: conservative 4*481.347588=1925.390352s, cap-based exposure 4*1203.368969=4813.475876s. Reserve at least 8000s for any failed attempts, host forward/backward/masking checks, attribution diagnostics and future paired repetitions of a consequential candidate and closest controls. Target three paired seeds if a later authorized host phase supports them; no seed/recipe override in these configs.',\n 'assessment_reserve':'Keep at least 10000s for freezing and host-managed final suite assessment/repetition; withheld scoring remains closed in this phase. Freeze source/config/checkpoint hashes, chosen candidates, DIN/closest controls and assessment commands before any assessment request. No held-out request is made here.',\n 'stop_rule':'Do not train or install in the coding jail. Only prepare four standalone modules and proposal; host executes under the frozen interface. Each reference rerun, check, control, failure or retry consumes the same ledger. Do not exceed the host 1203.368969s per-trial cap, campaign deadline or remaining budget; unknown execution state does not authorize a duplicate. Stop on integrity failure or when no useful experiment fits while preserving reserves. A nonpositive contrast is a valid result and cannot justify changing the protocol.'\n}}\ncontrasts=[\n ('mass_sum versus count_scaled','mass_sum: unnormalized sigmoid weights, total mass M[b,f]','count_scaled: identical relative sigmoid weights with target-independent mass N/2','Target-dependent mass beyond count','Follow with paired host-managed repetitions of mass_sum/count_scaled and normalized reference if click gain >=0.001; report residual optimization confounds.','If count scaling explains the gain, report that evidence and do not attribute value to target-dependent intensity.'),\n ('mass_sum versus mass_normalized','mass_sum retains total sigmoid activation mass','mass_normalized divides identical gates by their total mass','Retaining magnitude','Use count_scaled contrast to discriminate count from target-specific effects before claiming mechanism support.','Retain negative result and consider the unchanged projection bottleneck only in a subsequent prospective study.'),\n ('count_scaled versus mass_normalized','count_scaled multiplies the normalized summary by N/2','mass_normalized has unit total weight for nonempty histories','History-count-linked scaling','Describe a count-linked or optimization effect; target intensity requires the separate primary contrast.','Do not assume every unnormalized-pooling benefit comes from count.'),\n ('mass_normalized versus softmax_reference','Normalized sigmoid kernel','Original masked softmax kernel','Kernel replacement','If only this comparison wins, attribute provisional support to the kernel family, not magnitude.','A null kernel effect makes the mass contrasts easier to interpret but does not prove an intensity mechanism.'),\n ('softmax_reference versus g0-r0000-field_global','Faithful from-scratch rerun of the same reference pathway','Committed parent trial and source/config identity','Execution consistency','Use both receipts as provenance; a same-seed rerun is not an independent repetition.','Investigate implementation/protocol identity; suspend causal interpretation of the whole suite if mismatch is material.')\n]\nfor name,intervention,control,purpose,yes,no in contrasts:\n study['contrasts'].append({'parent_ref':'g0-r0000-field_global','intervention':name+': '+intervention,'control':control,'held_fixed':['Inputs, official_offline visibility, all labels, history limit 100, full split/scorers', 'Same field retrieval, no feedback grouping, log counts and absence flags', 'All trainable tensors, initialization order, parameter counts, token/readout structure', 'One pass, seed 20262027, batch 8192, split optimizers/rates, BCE sum, precision and host limits'], 'remaining_confounds':[purpose+' is tested under finite single-pass optimization; normalization also changes gradients and activation scale.','Single seed, no paired user-cluster bootstrap interface in this proposal phase; external-work equivalence unverified.'], 'decision_if_supported':yes, 'decision_if_not_supported':no})\nnotes={\n 'mass_sum':'Primary candidate. Bounded sigmoid gates are summed without unit normalization; target-conditioned field mass is retained. Compare first with count_scaled, then mass_normalized. No new tensors; not an exact DIN reproduction.',\n 'mass_normalized':'Matched kernel control. Same sigmoid gates normalized by gate sum; unit total weight for nonempty retrieval. Isolates mass retention from switching softmax to sigmoid.',\n 'count_scaled':'Matched count control. Same normalized sigmoid weights multiplied by visible exposure count/2, matching sum weights at zero scores. Target-independent scale controls a count explanation; global-scale and optimization differences remain.',\n 'softmax_reference':'Faithful contemporaneous parent reference; exact original masked softmax path and initialization/tensors. Verify against g0-r0000-field_global. Same-seed rerun is reproducibility evidence, not an independent mechanism replicate.'}\nproposal={'study':study,'parent_trial':'g0-r0000-field_global','variants':[{'name':name,'file':file,'role':role,'config':dict(shared,pooling_mode=mode),'note':notes[name]} for name,file,role,mode in specs], 'knowledge_assessments':[\n {'knowledge_id':'unirank-method-hiformer','decision':'used','rationale':'Read the pinned benchmark forward/tokenizer and the primary benchmark summary. History pooling followed by static/history/task token interaction is an inherited structural reference, not new local proof. The prior local model already has this broad pattern; original HiFormer text is unavailable.','transformed_mechanism':'Retain inherited post-pooling unified interaction, but change only field-wise retrieval normalization to test intensity-carrying summary units. No hierarchical HiFormer block is copied or newly introduced.','variant_names':[s[0] for s in specs],'nearest_work_difference':'Our inherited per-field target/context queries, five global summaries, shared small blocks and direct summary/static skip differ from the inspected event pooling and heterogeneous HiFormer blocks. The magnitude motivation is known from DIN; novelty unverified.','falsifier':'A mass_sum failure against matched normalization/count controls gives no support for the proposed intensity change; no claim about original HiFormer effectiveness follows.'},\n {'knowledge_id':'unirank-method-tokenmixer','decision':'considered','rationale':'Read pinned benchmark forward/tokenizer and mixing blocks plus benchmark section 2.3. Global/group tokenization and reversible mixing would change multiple mechanisms beyond the normalization limitation currently under test. Original method paper detail unavailable.','transformed_mechanism':'None; retain the existing interaction stack and token layout.','variant_names':[],'nearest_work_difference':'No reversible token transport, global/chunk tokenizer or mean-token readout is added. This is not a TokenMixer reproduction or local comparison.','falsifier':'A later capacity/cost-matched tokenizer replacement could support revisiting this alternative; current pooling controls cannot establish its ineffectiveness.'}\n]}\nPath('proposal.json').write_text(json.dumps(proposal,indent=2)+'\\\\n')\nprint('Recorded prospective four-arm study in proposal.json before implementation.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
